            "text": "[{'url': 'https://www.topgear.com/car-reviews/mercedes-benz/e-class/interior', 'content': 'Mercedes-Benz E-Class Interior Layout & Technology | Top Gear Advertisement Close advertisement United Kingdom United States Skip to main content United Kingdom United States Input your search term Search Clear Reviews News All news USA news Electric Advice Gaming Watches Hot Lists More Retro Big Reads Videos TV Show Satire Podcast Subscribe to Top Gear Magazine Hot Hatch SEARCH Input your search term Search Clear Home Car Reviews Mercedes-Benz E Class Interior Overview Driving Interior Buying Specs & Prices Car Review Mercedes-Benz E-Class review Prices from 56,055 - 77,555 Leasing offers from 681 p/m External link to with Leasing.com 7 TopGear.com Published: 13 May 2025 Read why you can trust our independent reviews Overview Driving Interior Buying Specs & Prices Skip 19 photos in the image carousel and continue reading Turn on Javascript to see all the available pictures. 3 / 19 Advertisement Interior What is it like on the inside? Its quite flashy in here, in that unique Mercedes way that looks and feels expensive but not terribly elegant. Do you know what we mean? The silver trim pieces are a bit chintzy for our liking and when you kill the ignition youre left with a collage of fingerprints across the massive glass panel. No least because theres nowhere to brace your other fingers while prodding. Hardly luxurious. More likely to have you reaching for the Mr Muscle. Lit up, especially at night, it looks fabulous when you get in, but quickly becomes irritating on the move. As in so many other cars, you find yourself relying on the lane keep assistance as you figure out what the hell is going on in the touchscreen. Not luxurious. Advertisement - Page continues below Meanwhile the last surviving buttons have been deleted in favour of haptic steering wheel touchpads, which either dont do what you want or do what you dont want when you dont want it. These are beyond frustrating. They colour your experience of the whole car. Hows the rest of the driving environment? Gone is the quartet of vents in the middle of the dash, replaced by a sleek band that wraps over the top of the screens. Meanwhile, the centre console no longer rises up to meet the dash, so it does feel less cluttered in here. The driving position is good, the seats are very comfortable even after hours at the wheel. Its a pity you cant unbolt them when you get home and stick them in front of the telly. Do you care about visibility? Lets assume you do. The view out the mirrors is okay but no more than that. Luckily Mercedes makes up for this with an armada of cameras and sensors to track potential dangers. Lets get back to the tech. Its incredible. Integrated 5G conference calling, power-operated vents, real-time nav graphic overlays, ambient lighting that beats in time with your music Angry Birds (nope, not kidding). Not to mention active steering that can change lanes and overtake for you with a single press of the indicator stalk. Advertisement - Page continues below But when youve got so much going on to distract you, thats hardly in keeping with a car thats supposed to take the stress out of driving. And its not like the systems work beautifully. For instance the lane keep isnt as good as BMW s (although it is significantly better than Land Rover s). Anything else to get off your chest? Yes. The column stalks feel cheap and nasty. How on earth did such flimsy parts make the cut? Pull down into D and it feels like the right hand stalk might snap off. And breathe. Is it roomy? Very much so. The E-Class is 40mm longer and 22mm wider than before, while the wheelbase has grown by 22mm also. That means more space inside for torsos, limbs and extremities, though its a four-seater only for adults: you might squeeze a child into the middle back seat if you have to. Although theyll only whine about the transmission tunnel. At 540 litres the boot is exactly the same size as the old-gen car, but be warned about the PHEV: the higher floor means theres just 370 litres t', 'images': [], 'rank': 1}, {'url': 'https://www.caranddriver.com/mercedes-benz/e-class', 'content': \"Skip to Content Global Nav Search SUBSCRIBE SIGN IN RESEARCH CARS EXPERT REVIEWS SHOPPING TOOLS WHATS MY CAR WORTH? EXPERT-TESTED GEAR NEWS + STORIES MAINTENANCE HomeMercedes-BenzE-Class 2025 2024 2023 2022 2021 2020 2019 2018 2017 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-Class 10 / 10 C/D RATING MARC URBANO|CAR AND DRIVER View Photos MSRP $63,600$76,290 Car and Driver Accolades What's this? Editors' Choice 10Best AT A GLANCE OVERVIEW RANK WHAT'S NEW PRICING ENGINE & PERFORMANCE 0-60 TIME FUEL ECONOMY INTERIOR INFOTAINMENT & TECH SAFETY WARRANTY & MAINTENANCE HIGHS Plush, deeply comfortable cabin, potent and smooth six-cylinder, sophisticated tech-forward interior. LOWS Limited cargo capacity, capacitive-touch controls can be frustrating, pricier than rivals. VERDICT The Mercedes-Benz E-class is anelegant and luxurious choice among midsize luxury sedana compelling high-end machine that rolls with a healthy dose of technology. Specs SEE ALL SPECS EPA MPG 2528 Combined Seating 5 Seats Cargo Capacity 13 Cubic Feet Powertrain Gas Drivetrain All-Wheel Drive, Rear-Wheel Drive Limited Warranty 4 Years/50,000 Miles By Mark Takahashi Overview The 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-class is a rich amalgam of refinement, comfort, and innovationone of the best examples of the luxury car breed. It's very much the midsize interpretation of the stately and posh S-Classwhich is saying a lotwith a corresponding increase in agility and maneuverability. Compared to the BMW 5 series and Audi A6, the E-Class is more luxurious and opulent inside, with a handsomely renderedif complicatedinfotainment system delivering all of the newest tech features. It's not as sporty or athletic as the BMW or Audi, but for most luxury clients, that's not a priority. Still, it displays enough finesse and style to win a spot on our 2025 10Best and 2025 Editors' Choice lists. For the rare driver desiring more performance, the new Mercedes-AMG E53 plug-in hybrid model with 604 horsepower should easily satisfy. We review that model separately. Where This Vehicle Ranks #1 in Best Luxury Mid-Size Cars 1 Mercedes-Benz E-class STARTING AT: $63,600 2 Genesis G80 STARTING AT: $58,595 3 Audi A7 STARTING AT: $73,295 4 Audi A6 STARTING AT: $59,395 5 BMW 5-series STARTING AT: $59,875 6 Volvo S90 STARTING AT: $59,495 New! Compare the 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-class with the top vehicles in this segment with our new compare tool. COMPARE THE TOP CARS What's New for 2025? This year, the E-class gets a new base model in the form of a rear-wheel-drive E350 four-cylinder. Mercedes' 4Matic all-wheel drive is available as an option and remains standard on the E450. A new center-mounted airbag is standard across the lineup and deploys between the two front-seat occupants in the event of an accident. Patagonia Red is a new exterior color option and a faux-leather steering wheel is also now available. Pricing and Which One to Buy The price of the 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-class starts at $63,600 and goes up to $76,290 depending on the trim and options. E350 $63,600 E350 Pinnacle $67,890 E450 $72,000 E450 Pinnacle $76,290 0 $25k $50k $75k $100k $125k In addition to the choice of four-cylinder E350 or six-cylinder E450 engine choices, the E-class also offers a few different trim levels with varying levels of equipment: Premium and Pinnacle. We'd stick with the four-cylinder powertrain in the E350 and spend the savings on upgrading to the Pinnacle trim, as it adds a lot of luxuries. Pinnacle models come with a 17-speaker Burmester stereo system, a head-up display, noise-insulating window glass, an illuminated grille, and ambient interior lighting. The E350 is the only model that does not come with 4Matic all-wheel-drive as standard, so we recommend adding it for only $2,500, especially for those who desire more performance or traction in inclement weather. And well forgive you if you choose to shell out the extra coin for the E450 in order to get its silky inline-six-cylinder engine; its velvety smoothness enhance\", 'images': [], 'rank': 2}, {'url': 'https://www.motortrend.com/cars/mercedes-benz/e-class', 'content': \"2025 Mercedes-Benz E-Class Review: Expert Insights, Pricing, and Trims MotorTrend MotorTrend News Reviews Buyer's Guide Videos The Future Events Ask MOTORTREND Beta Home / Cars / Mercedes-Benz / E-Class 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-Class in Luxury Midsize Sedans 8.6 /10 Rating 2026 2025 2024 2023 2022 More Compare Price $62,450 - $88,000 8.6 /10 27 Photos Specs See Local Listings Price $62,450 - $88,000 See Local Listings Pros Nimble handling Smooth suspension Well-tuned driver assist systems Cons Laggardly brake pedal Cabin gets stuffy during auto-stops Interruptions from voice assistant 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-Class Expert Review Reviewed by Alex Leanse Fresh from its fifth-generation redesign for 2024, the 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-Class returns with a new entry-level rear-wheel-drive variant and a powerful E53 Hybrid 4Matic+ from Mercedes-AMG. Stuffed with high-tech appointments and offered with sophisticated drivetrains, the Mercedes-Benz E-Class goes to bat with other midsize luxury sedans including the Audi A6, BMW 5 Series, and Genesis G80. For its all-around greatness, we named the Mercedes-Benz E-Class our 2025 Car of the Year. Whats New Rear-wheel-drive E350 base model arrives High-output Mercedes-AMG E53 4Matic+ PHEV introduced Center airbag between front seats becomes standard Special-order Patagonia Red paint offered Leather-free MB-Tex interior upholstery option now extends to steering wheel What We Think The 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-Class may recall visions of old-school luxury to new car shoppers, but the midsize sedan has evolved with the times. This is a thoroughly high-tech car , complete with large displays, advanced safety equipment, and novel powertrains. Forget any associations with boats or barges, too; the E-Class models weve tested have exhibited nimble handling, good body control, and a lightweight feel on curvy roads. There are two new variants of the E-Class for 2025. A less expensive rear-drive E350 now serves as the entry-level model. Also, the Mercedes-AMG E53 Hybrid 4Matic+ is offered as a powerful PHEV and the sportiest E-Class sedan until the E63 is added to the range. A note for wagon lovers: Mercedes-Benz still offers the E-Class in its lifted All-Terrain body style , which we heartily recommend to those who prefer to split the difference between a car and an SUV. We have very few qualms with the latest iteration of E-Class. A somewhat languid brake pedal takes away from the total driving confidence wed otherwise have when piloting the Benz in our testing. The vehicles abundant tech gives way to minor annoyances, like a voice assistant thats a little eager to step in and help. We applaud the 2025 Mercedes-AMG E53 Hybrid 4Matic+'s super sedan handling , but its behavior in everyday driving is uncannily ordinary. In addition, auto stop-start cuts the HVAC system in non-hybrid models, leaving the cabin feeling stuffy at stoplights. That said, the fuel-saving powertrain feature is now virtually imperceptible. The Mercedes-Benz E-Class is a reliable constant, always what you need when you need it, nothing more, nothing less. So fond are we of the 10th-generation E-Class, its our 2025 MotorTrend Car of the Year . Performance and MPGs There are now three powertrain options for the 2025 Mercedes-Benz E-Class. Regardless of configuration, the E-Class receives a nine-speed automatic transmission. E350 models, offered with rear-wheel drive and the automakers 4Matic all-wheel drive, receive a 2.0-liter four-cylinder engine and an integrated starter-generator. This mild hybrid system develops 255 hp and 295 lb-ft of torque, with an available 20 hp and 148 lb-ft of torque on tap for an added boost. In our testing , a 2024 Mercedes-Benz E350 4Matic accelerated from 0 to 60 mph in 5.9 seconds. The EPA rates the E350 4Matic at 24/33 mpg city/highway with all-wheel drive. RWD models are rated at 25/33 mpg. Those in search of a more powerful E-Class can step up to the E450 4Matic, which produces 375 hp and 369 lb-ft of torque\", 'images': [], 'rank': 3}, {'url': 'https://cars.usnews.com/cars-trucks/mercedes-benz/e-class/interior', 'content': '[error] Timeout while extracting content', 'rank': 4}, {'url': 'https://www.statecollegemercedes.com/blog/2024/december/10/2025-mercedes-benz-e-class-interior.htm', 'content': '[error] Timeout while extracting content', 'rank': 5}, {'url': 'https://www.autocarindia.com/car-reviews/new-mercedes-e-class-review-has-the-best-gotten-better-433240', 'content': '[not extracted] Content limit reached', 'rank': 6}]",
